# The next instrument for #72, NOT merged by default.
#
# gemini-kbuild.sh globs configs/*.config, so the ".optional" suffix keeps this
# out of every build. To use it:
#
#     cp configs/gemini-pagealloc.config.optional configs/gemini-pagealloc.config
#     scripts/gemini-kbuild.sh <n>     # then pack and flash as usual
#
# WHY IT IS NOT ON BY DEFAULT
#
# DEBUG_PAGEALLOC unmaps every freed page, so each alloc/free does page-table
# work and a TLB flush. On this SoC that is a large, always-on cost, and this
# machine is somebody's desktop -- #55 just spent a session making it idle
# cheaper, and turning it 2-5x slower is not a change to make on its owner's
# behalf without asking.
#
# WHY IT IS THE RIGHT NEXT INSTRUMENT ANYWAY
#
# #72 is a wild pointer whose *writer* has never been identified; every capture
# is of the next reader tripping over the damage, minutes or hours later.
# DEBUG_PAGEALLOC faults the writer, at the instant of the write, with a call
# trace naming it.
#
# The standing objection was that a fault it caught would still have to be read
# somewhere. That objection is now gone twice over: pstore was never broken
# (2026-08-27), and the printing path is measured good -- the identical fault
# taken deliberately from process, workqueue and softirq context prints its
# report in full (2026-08-28, tools/oopsgen/).
#
# HOW TO USE IT ONCE FLASHED
#
# The detector for the corruption is any cache walk, so pair this with a SLOW
# periodic one -- one `sync; echo 3 > /proc/sys/vm/drop_caches` every few
# minutes, for hours, on an otherwise normal machine. Not 60 in four minutes:
# that was tried and a fresh boot has nothing planted yet to trip over.
CONFIG_DEBUG_PAGEALLOC=y
CONFIG_DEBUG_PAGEALLOC_ENABLE_DEFAULT=y
